WEBVTT

00:00.090 --> 00:02.190
Instructor: Okay, so I'll keep this short.

00:02.190 --> 00:03.510
Now that we have some code

00:03.510 --> 00:06.450
some repo up on GitHub that actually has contents

00:06.450 --> 00:07.710
it's not empty anymore.

00:07.710 --> 00:10.410
Even if the contents absolutely suck

00:10.410 --> 00:12.690
they're garbage like this repo.

00:12.690 --> 00:15.210
I wanna walk through what we're actually looking at.

00:15.210 --> 00:16.770
So of course we see the files

00:16.770 --> 00:18.300
the actual contents here, right?

00:18.300 --> 00:19.830
We've got chapter one.

00:19.830 --> 00:24.060
We can look at that and inspect and read.

00:24.060 --> 00:25.410
Sure.

00:25.410 --> 00:28.170
We can also switch branches.

00:28.170 --> 00:30.810
So we only have two right now, empty, which was

00:30.810 --> 00:33.750
that branch I made where almost everything was deleted.

00:33.750 --> 00:35.013
And then master.

00:36.240 --> 00:38.700
Notice if I go back to empty, it tells us, you know

00:38.700 --> 00:42.150
this branch has one commit that master does not have.

00:42.150 --> 00:44.950
But it also is missing one commit that master has

00:45.960 --> 00:48.300
we can see here, most recent push

00:48.300 --> 00:50.642
to this branch happened four minutes ago.

00:50.642 --> 00:52.800
And we can also

00:52.800 --> 00:55.260
if we go back to master, it doesn't really matter.

00:55.260 --> 00:58.770
We can see the commits listed here, the most

00:58.770 --> 01:01.560
recent commit that affected each one of these files.

01:01.560 --> 01:03.210
So when I made chapter four

01:03.210 --> 01:06.030
that text file that was part of the commit

01:06.030 --> 01:10.440
begin chapter four, when I added headings to files.

01:10.440 --> 01:12.390
You can see that's the most recent change

01:12.390 --> 01:16.350
to this and this and this one and the outline.

01:16.350 --> 01:19.383
If you recall a while ago, I added those headings in,

01:20.280 --> 01:22.560
anyway if I wanna see all the commits

01:22.560 --> 01:25.860
I can click here where it says 13 commits

01:25.860 --> 01:29.340
and I can see just a visual representation of each commit.

01:29.340 --> 01:31.410
So here's where I began.

01:31.410 --> 01:33.180
Yeah, if you have multiple pages

01:33.180 --> 01:36.030
like lots and lots of commits, you can go between pages.

01:36.030 --> 01:38.250
But this is my most recent commit.

01:38.250 --> 01:40.650
If I click, what do we see?

01:40.650 --> 01:43.050
Well, I just started with these two files

01:43.050 --> 01:45.993
outline.txt and characters.txt.

01:47.460 --> 01:49.050
If I look at a different commit

01:49.050 --> 01:52.110
how about when I...

01:52.110 --> 01:53.160
what did I do?

01:53.160 --> 01:55.720
How about removed extra blank lines in chapter two

01:56.580 --> 02:00.210
if I scroll down, you'll see highlighted in red.

02:00.210 --> 02:01.860
Those are the two lines that were removed

02:01.860 --> 02:03.270
and this is all that was affected.

02:03.270 --> 02:05.667
This one file, right?

02:05.667 --> 02:08.130
We see some other stuff like when I renamed Gatsby

02:08.130 --> 02:11.520
to Catsby, lots of files impacted.

02:11.520 --> 02:13.200
You can see all those impacts here.

02:13.200 --> 02:14.520
This line changed.

02:14.520 --> 02:18.810
Gatsby became Catsby and all these other files too.

02:18.810 --> 02:21.300
So I can see all those commits on the master branch.

02:21.300 --> 02:23.730
The most recent one is begin chapter four.

02:23.730 --> 02:26.700
If I switch to empty, the most recent commit is

02:26.700 --> 02:30.540
delete most things, which in the last video you saw

02:30.540 --> 02:32.640
I deleted quite a few files.

02:32.640 --> 02:34.530
And on each of these commits, I see the commit

02:34.530 --> 02:36.300
message obviously.

02:36.300 --> 02:39.270
And then I also see the commit hash.

02:39.270 --> 02:42.342
And then for some of them that have much longer commits

02:42.342 --> 02:44.040
like this one, right?

02:44.040 --> 02:46.080
I can see the entire commit message.

02:46.080 --> 02:48.270
This is when I demonstrated, you know, how to set up

02:48.270 --> 02:50.280
VS code to make a commit message.

02:50.280 --> 02:51.630
That's much longer.

02:51.630 --> 02:53.490
So I can see the whole commit message here.

02:53.490 --> 02:55.410
Then I can see the files impacted

02:55.410 --> 02:58.590
and I can leave a comment if I want to.

02:58.590 --> 03:00.720
Probably not going to because I'm not collaborating

03:00.720 --> 03:01.553
with anyone.

03:01.553 --> 03:02.386
But if I want to

03:02.386 --> 03:05.400
I can leave comments here and I don't know

03:05.400 --> 03:08.190
revisit them and come back and look at my comments.

03:08.190 --> 03:09.900
But this is actually a very important part of

03:09.900 --> 03:11.880
of discussing changes

03:11.880 --> 03:13.980
once you start working with other people.

03:13.980 --> 03:16.140
And that's pretty much all we need to cover for now.

03:16.140 --> 03:18.810
Eventually we'll learn about things like poll requests

03:18.810 --> 03:21.330
and how to make poll requests, how to compare things.

03:21.330 --> 03:22.890
We are not at that point yet.

03:22.890 --> 03:25.920
That's gonna come up when we talk about workflows.

03:25.920 --> 03:27.960
But yeah, that's pretty much it.

03:27.960 --> 03:29.807
Switch branches here, that's probably the most

03:29.807 --> 03:32.418
hidden thing that sometimes students miss.

03:32.418 --> 03:34.650
If you don't see your changes showing up.

03:34.650 --> 03:37.506
And if you have multiple branches, make sure you switch

03:37.506 --> 03:39.961
to the correct branch to see those changes.

03:39.961 --> 03:41.013
Okay.
